# Route Constants Usage

## Core Principle: Never Use Hardcoded Route Paths

All route paths must be defined in `packages/core/constants/routes.ts` and referenced through the exported constants.

## Route Constants Structure

### Nested Route Structure

Routes are organized in a nested, hierarchical structure for better organization and type safety:

```typescript
import { ROUTES } from '@app/core/constants/routes'

// Dashboard routes - nested structure
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.path                    // "/dashboard"
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.path             // "/dashboard/profile"
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL.path     // "/dashboard/profile/general"
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.EMPLOYMENT.path  // "/dashboard/profile/employment"
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.SETTINGS.path            // "/dashboard/settings"
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.SETTINGS.GENERAL.path    // "/dashboard/settings/general"

// Office routes - nested structure
ROUTES.OFFICE.path                        // "/office"
ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.path                    // "/office/cms"
ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.JOBS.path               // "/office/cms/jobs"
ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.JOBS.CREATE.path        // "/office/cms/jobs/create"
ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.JOBS.EDIT.path          // "/office/cms/jobs/:id/edit"
ROUTES.OFFICE.ATS.CHECKS.path             // "/office/ats/checks"

// Auth routes - nested structure
ROUTES.AUTH.LOGIN.path                    // "/auth"
ROUTES.AUTH.CONFIRM.path                  // "/auth/confirm"
ROUTES.AUTH.SUCCESS.path                   // "/auth/success"
```

### Legacy Route Constants (Deprecated)

The following are deprecated but still available for backward compatibility:

```typescript
import { 
  DASHBOARD_ROUTES, 
  OFFICE_ROUTES, 
  AUTH_ROUTES 
} from '@app/core/constants/routes'

// These still work but use nested structure internally
DASHBOARD_ROUTES.INDEX.path        // "/dashboard"
OFFICE_ROUTES.JOBS.path            // "/office/cms/jobs"
AUTH_ROUTES.INDEX.path              // "/auth"
```

## Usage Patterns

### ❌ Bad - Hardcoded Paths

```typescript
// DON'T DO THIS
<Link href="/dashboard">Dashboard</Link>
<Link href="/office/jobs">Manage Jobs</Link>

router.push('/dashboard')
router.replace('/office')

const isActive = pathname === '/dashboard'
```

### ✅ Good - Using Nested Route Constants

```typescript
// DO THIS - Use nested structure
import { ROUTES } from '@app/core/constants/routes'

<Link href={ROUTES.DASHBOARD.path}>Dashboard</Link>
<Link href={ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.JOBS.path}>Manage Jobs</Link>
<Link href={ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL.path}>Profile</Link>

router.push(ROUTES.DASHBOARD.path)
router.replace(ROUTES.OFFICE.path)

const isActive = pathname === ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL.path
```

### Using Route Metadata

```typescript
// Access route metadata from nested structure
const title = ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.title        // "Profile"
const path = ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.path          // "/dashboard/profile"
const isProtected = ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.protected // true
const icon = ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.icon          // ComponentType (if set)
```

## Adding New Routes

When adding new routes, update `packages/core/constants/routes.ts`:

```typescript
// Add to nested ROUTES_CONFIG structure
const ROUTES_CONFIG = {
  // ... existing routes
  
  DASHBOARD: {
    // ... existing dashboard routes
    
    NEW_SECTION: {
      path: "/dashboard/new-section",
      title: "New Section",
      protected: true,
      exact: false,
      SUBSECTION: {
        path: "/dashboard/new-section/subsection",
        title: "Subsection",
        protected: true,
        exact: true,
      },
    },
  },
} as const

// Access via nested structure
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.NEW_SECTION.path           // "/dashboard/new-section"
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.NEW_SECTION.SUBSECTION.path // "/dashboard/new-section/subsection"
```

### Route Configuration Properties

- `path`: The route path (required)
- `title`: Display title (required)
- `protected`: Whether route requires authentication (optional, default: false)
- `roles`: Array of required roles for access (optional)
- `icon`: Icon component (optional)
- `exact`: Whether path must match exactly (optional, default: false)
- `hidden`: Whether to hide from navigation (optional, default: false)

## Special Cases

### Root Path

```typescript
// Root path is always "/"
if (href === "/") {
  return pathname === "/" || pathname === "/index"
}
```

### Path Matching and Helper Functions

Use route constants and helper functions for path operations:

```typescript
import { ROUTES, matchesRoute, isActiveRoute, getBreadcrumbs } from '@app/core/constants/routes'

// Exact path matching
const dashboardPath = ROUTES.DASHBOARD.path
if (href === dashboardPath) {
  return pathname === dashboardPath
}

// Smart path matching (handles dynamic segments and exact flag)
matchesRoute('/dashboard/profile/general', ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL) // true
matchesRoute('/dashboard/profile/general', ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE) // true (prefix match)

// Active route checking
isActiveRoute('/dashboard/profile/general', ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE) // true

// Breadcrumb generation
const breadcrumbs = getBreadcrumbs('/dashboard/profile/general')
// Returns: [ROUTES.DASHBOARD, ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE, ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL]
```

## Benefits

1. **Type Safety** - TypeScript will catch typos and missing routes
2. **Single Source of Truth** - All paths defined in one place
3. **Easier Refactoring** - Change path in one place, updates everywhere
4. **Better IDE Support** - Autocomplete and navigation
5. **Documentation** - Route metadata (title, description) in one place

## Enforcement

- **ALWAYS import ROUTES** when working with navigation
- **NEVER use string literals** for route paths (except in routes.ts itself)
- **ALWAYS use `.path`** property to get the actual path string
- **USE nested structure** for route access (e.g., `ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL.path`)
- **DOCUMENT new routes** with proper titles and descriptions
- **USE helper functions** (`buildPath`, `matchesRoute`, `getBreadcrumbs`) for route operations

## Migration

When finding hardcoded paths or old flat route references:

1. Identify the path and its purpose
2. Check if it exists in `routes.ts` nested structure
3. If not, add it to `routes.ts` with proper nested structure
4. Replace hardcoded string or flat reference with nested route constant
5. Update from `ROUTES.DASHBOARD_PROFILE_GENERAL` to `ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL`
6. Use `buildPath()` for dynamic routes instead of string replacement
7. Verify the change works correctly

### Migration Examples

```typescript
// Old flat structure (deprecated)
ROUTES.DASHBOARD_PROFILE_GENERAL.path
ROUTES.OFFICE_CMS_JOBS_CREATE.path

// New nested structure
ROUTES.DASHBOARD.PROFILE.GENERAL.path
ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.JOBS.CREATE.path

// Dynamic routes
buildPath(ROUTES.OFFICE.CMS.JOBS.EDIT, { id: '123' }) // "/office/cms/jobs/123/edit"
```
